.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt TELINIT 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm telinit
.Nd change the runlevel
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Ar runlevel
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
asks
.Xr init 8
for a runlevel.
The request goes down
.Pa /run/initctl ,
and everything that follows from it - stopping the services the old runlevel
started, starting the ones the new one asks for, taking the machine down -
belongs to
.Xr init 8 .
.Nm
returns as soon as the request is written; it does not wait for the runlevel to
be reached.
.Pp
The argument is one character:
.Bl -tag -width "1, S, s" -offset indent
.It Cm 0
halt or power the system off.
.It Cm 1 , S , s
single user: a root shell on the console.
.Cm 1
runs the rc scripts on the way, so the services are stopped;
.Cm S
does not.
.It Cm 2-5
multi user.
.Cm 3
is the Sowa default.
.It Cm 6
reboot.
.It Cm Q , q
reread
.Xr inittab 5
without changing the runlevel.
Nothing is restarted by this: an entry whose command changed is left alone
until it exits on its own, and one that has been deleted is stopped only when
it does.
.It Cm a , b , c
run the
.Cm ondemand
entries for that letter.
These are not runlevels: the current runlevel does not change, and nothing
else is started or stopped.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-t seconds"
.It Fl t Ar seconds
How long a process gets between the
.Dv SIGTERM
that asks it to leave the runlevel and the
.Dv SIGKILL
that makes it.
This is passed to
.Xr init 8
with the request and applies to that shutdown only.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/run/initctl" -compact
.It Pa /run/initctl
the FIFO the request is written to.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A request that cannot be delivered - no
.Pa /run/initctl ,
or nothing reading it - is a failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Stop the services and keep the console:
.Pp
.Dl telinit 1
.Pp
Go back to the default runlevel:
.Pp
.Dl telinit 3
.Pp
Apply an edited inittab:
.Pp
.Dl telinit q
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr inittab 5 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr runlevel 8 ,
.Xr shutdown 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Only root can change the runlevel.
.Pp
.Ic init Ar runlevel
run by anyone but process 1 does the same thing: it forwards the request and
exits, exactly as System V's did.
